========================================================================
                      PORTABLE ED TORTURE SUITE
========================================================================

This directory contains language-neutral, black-box tests for implementations
of the POSIX ed utility.  The data is licensed under 0BSD and contains no
material from private conformance products.

The suite is designed to be reused.  It does not import tsed code, assume an
editor implementation language, or require that the editor executable be
named ed.  The supplied runners use Bun for process and PTY support, but any
program capable of reading JSON and launching a process can consume the data.

The suite is a torture and regression corpus.  Passing it is strong evidence
about the behaviors it exercises, but is not a formal POSIX certification.


COLLECTIONS
------------------------------------------------------------------------

cases.json is the frozen version 1 corpus.  It contains 125 small pipe-driven
cases with exact input, output, and status expectations.  Its public fields
are:

    name        stable descriptive identifier
    arguments   command-line arguments passed to the editor
    stdin       exact bytes supplied to standard input
    stdout      exact expected standard output
    stderr      exact expected standard error
    status      expected process exit status

cases-v2.json is the POSIX.1-2024 torture corpus.  It adds generated behavior
matrices, file fixtures, signals, PTYs, locale prerequisites, multiple
acceptable outcomes, and byte-level comparisons.  Its public format is
specified by schema-v2.json.

requirements.json maps paraphrased POSIX requirements to exact public case
names.  It does not depend on tsed's private unit tests.  Its format is
specified by requirements-schema-v1.json.

Generated JSON is the public interface.  Generator TypeScript is an authoring
tool and can change without changing the corpus format.


RUNNING AN EDITOR
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Build tsed and run both collections:

    bun run test:portable

Run version 1 against another editor:

    bun test/portable_runner.ts /path/to/ed

Run the torture corpus against another editor:

    bun test/portable_v2_runner.ts --editor /path/to/ed

Select a case-name substring or suppress per-case output:

    bun test/portable_v2_runner.ts --editor /path/to/ed --case substitute
    bun test/portable_v2_runner.ts --editor /path/to/ed --quiet

Exercise a missing-capability result path explicitly:

    bun test/portable_v2_runner.ts \
        --editor /path/to/ed \
        --disable-capability utf8-locale \
        --case torture/locale

Use a copied or modified corpus:

    bun test/portable_v2_runner.ts \
        --editor /path/to/ed \
        --corpus /path/to/cases-v2.json

Produce a machine-readable report:

    bun test/portable_v2_runner.ts --editor /path/to/ed --json

The npm package includes both runners, the PTY harness, schemas, maps, and
corpora.  A package consumer can run:

    bun node_modules/@wordgitet/tsed/test/portable_v2_runner.ts \
        --editor ./my-ed


RESULTS
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The torture runner uses its own result vocabulary:

    OK             an expected outcome matched
    MISMATCH       editor behavior matched no expected outcome
    SKIP           a case prerequisite, such as a UTF-8 locale, was absent
    RUNNER_LIMIT   this runner could not provide a required facility
    HARNESS_ERROR  execution did not produce a trustworthy comparison

MISMATCH describes an observable difference, not its cause.  Confirm the
case against the cited POSIX requirement before changing an editor.  Reference
implementations are useful secondary evidence, but agreement among historical
editors does not override normative text.

The runner exits 1 when any MISMATCH is present.  It exits 2 when there is a
HARNESS_ERROR and no mismatch.  SKIP and RUNNER_LIMIT remain visible in the
report without being silently counted as successful executions.


VERSION 2 DATA
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each version 2 case contains:

    name            stable case identifier
    requirement     identifier from requirements.json
    mode            pipe or pty
    arguments       editor command-line arguments
    expect          one or more acceptable observable outcomes

Optional fields provide:

    environment     additional environment variables
    stdin           UTF-8 or base64 pipe input
    actions         PTY waits, writes, and signals
    fixtures        files created below a private temporary directory
    requires        runner capabilities needed by the case
    timeout_ms      case-specific execution limit

Recognized capabilities are posix-shell, pty, signals, and utf8-locale.  The
runner probes them once and records the probe results in JSON reports.

An expected process status can be an exact integer, nonzero, or any.  Streams
can be compared exactly or checked for required byte strings and minimum byte
counts.  Cases can verify exact files and assert that named files were not
created.

The token @TMP@ in UTF-8 strings expands to the private directory allocated
for one case.  The token @UTF8_LOCALE@ expands to the locale selected by the
UTF-8 capability probe.  Fixture and expected-file paths are relative and
cannot escape the case directory.


ADDING TORTURE CASES
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Do not edit generated JSON by hand.  Add compact rows or behavior matrices to
the generator sources, then regenerate all public artifacts:

    bun run generate:portable

Every case should isolate one observable rule or one deliberate interaction.
Expected results must come from public normative text.  Compare with GNU ed,
FreeBSD ed, or another implementation to catch mistaken expectations, but
record permitted alternatives instead of selecting a historical favorite.

New families should use stable names, reference a mapped requirement, avoid
implementation-specific diagnostic wording, and remain deterministic in a
fresh temporary directory.  PTY behavior must use PTY mode; a pipe is not a
substitute for terminal input.

Before contributing, run:

    bun run generate:portable
    bun run typecheck
    bun run check:style
    bun run test:portable

Format changes require a new corpus version.  Adding optional fields or new
cases can remain within version 2 when old version 2 consumers can continue to
read the data.
